66
00:05:10.641 --> 00:05:11.964
Quantity, and Value.

67
00:05:12.244 --> 00:05:13.332
Subscribers:

68
00:05:13.612 --> 00:05:17.729
400M (90% prepaid) (VERIFY market figures).

69
00:05:18.009 --> 00:05:21.529
Active data sessions at peak: ~150M.

70
00:05:21.809 --> 00:05:29.233
Charging requests (session start or update or end): ~1 to 2M per second at peak (§1.4).

71
00:05:29.513 --> 00:05:32.500
Voice calls at peak: ~10M concurrent.

72
00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:38.369
Recharges per day: ~20 to 30M; peaks on festival offers and month starts.

73
00:05:38.649 --> 00:05:41.700
Usage records (CDRs or UDRs) per day:

74
00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:43.175
Tens of billions.

75
00:05:43.455 --> 00:05:47.807
Postpaid bills per month: ~40M, spread across bill cycles.

76
00:05:49.157 --> 00:05:51.013
Estimation, charging load.

77
00:05:51.293 --> 00:05:52.146
Sessions:

78
00:05:52.426 --> 00:05:54.879
150M active data sessions.

79
00:05:55.159 --> 00:06:03.778
Each session gets a quota (for example, 50 MB or a time-validity window) and reports back when it is used or a timer expires.

80
00:06:04.058 --> 00:06:18.671
Update rate: if the average session reports every ~2 minutes, that is 150M or 120 s about 1.25M update requests per second, plus starts and stops, plus voice and SMS.

81
00:06:20.021 --> 00:06:31.051
Consequence: the OCS is a distributed, in-memory, strongly consistent balance store with sub-10 ms responses at over a million transactions per second.

82
00:06:31.331 --> 00:06:34.915
That is one of the most demanding transactional workloads anywhere.

83
00:06:35.195 --> 00:06:39.781
Tuning lever: quota size is a system tuning lever (§6.1).

84
00:06:40.061 --> 00:06:46.291
Bigger quotas mean fewer requests, but more risk of overuse or stranded balances between sessions.

