146
00:10:22.638 --> 00:10:23.555
Part 4.

147
00:10:23.835 --> 00:10:25.713
How It Works, Journeys End to End.

148
00:10:26.963 --> 00:10:29.800
Journey 1: opening a video on prepaid data.

149
00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:35.627
The subscriber has a pack with 2 GB of high-speed data per day, validity 28 days.

150
00:10:35.907 --> 00:10:55.811
Session start: the phone attaches and requests a data session;. the packet gateway (4G PGW, or 5G SMF controlling the UPF) creates the session;. it asks policy control (PCRF or PCF) for rules: speed, QoS class, and whether the subscriber is currently throttled;.

151
00:10:57.161 --> 00:11:03.177
policy control checks the subscriber's plan and usage state (from the OCS or its own counters).

152
00:11:03.457 --> 00:11:27.414
Initial quota request: the gateway sends a credit-control request (initial) to the OCS (Diameter Gy CCR-I in 4G, Nchf in 5G), identifying the subscriber and the service (rating group);. the OCS finds the subscriber's balance buckets (§5.3): today's high-speed data (2 GB, 1.2 GB left), plus any add-on packs;.

153
00:11:28.764 --> 00:11:44.103
it reserves a quota, for example, 50 MB (§6.1), deducting it from available balance as reserved, not consumed;. it replies within milliseconds with the granted units and a validity time (for example, 10 minutes).

154
00:11:44.383 --> 00:11:50.911
Usage: the gateway lets traffic flow until the 50 MB is used or the validity time expires.

155
00:11:51.191 --> 00:12:02.028
Update: then it sends a CCR-Update reporting the actual used units (say 48 MB). the OCS commits 48 MB from the reservation (consumed);.

156
00:12:03.378 --> 00:12:08.434
it releases the unused 2 MB;. it grants a new reservation of 50 MB.

157
00:12:08.714 --> 00:12:10.613
This repeats as the video plays.

158
00:12:10.893 --> 00:12:33.506
Threshold crossing: when the daily high-speed bucket reaches 100%: the OCS notifies policy control, which changes the session's policy to a lower speed (fair usage throttle, for example, 64 kbps; VERIFY plan terms) via the gateway (Gx or N7 update);. charging continues against the post-FUP allowance.

159
00:12:34.856 --> 00:12:44.563
If the plan has no post-FUP allowance and no other bucket, the OCS denies further quota and the gateway redirects the user to a recharge page.

160
00:12:44.843 --> 00:12:58.965
Session end: a CCR-Terminate reports final usage;. the OCS commits it and releases the remaining reservation;. a charging data record is written for mediation, customer usage history and analytics.

161
00:13:00.315 --> 00:13:12.411
Notifications: usage threshold events (50%, 90%, 100%) generate SMS or app notifications via a notification service (Ch 40).

162
00:13:12.691 --> 00:13:18.963
Midnight: a daily reset restores the high-speed allowance and lifts the throttle (§5.4).

163
00:13:20.313 --> 00:13:22.404
Journey 2: a VoLTE call.

164
00:13:22.684 --> 00:13:24.561
Call setup: the caller dials.

165
00:13:24.841 --> 00:13:38.473
The IMS sets up the call and asks the OCS for authorisation (Diameter Ro CCR-I or Nchf) with the called number, to determine the rate: on-net, off-net, national, international, premium.

166
00:13:38.753 --> 00:13:56.055
Rating and reservation: the OCS rates the call via the tariff (§6.3);. for an unlimited voice plan, it checks eligibility and fair-use rules, and grants time units (for example, 5 minutes);. for pay-per-use, it reserves money for the granted time.

167
00:13:57.405 --> 00:14:01.223
Updates: during the call, updates commit used time and grant more.

168
00:14:01.503 --> 00:14:12.106
Call end: final usage is committed, and a call record is generated for mediation (also needed for interconnect settlement if the call terminates on another operator).

169
00:14:13.456 --> 00:14:16.336
Journey 3: recharging with UPI in the app.

170
00:14:16.616 --> 00:14:20.285
Plan choice: the subscriber chooses a pack in the self-care app.

171
00:14:20.565 --> 00:14:25.685
The app shows eligible plans from the catalogue (by circle, current plan and offers).

172
00:14:25.965 --> 00:14:31.171
Payment: via UPI intent through the payment orchestration (Ch 45).

173
00:14:31.451 --> 00:14:45.381
On success (callback), the recharge service: creates the order;. applies the plan to the subscriber in the OCS (new buckets with validity, or extension of validity rules);. updates policy control if speeds change;.

174
00:14:46.731 --> 00:14:48.011
sends confirmation.

175
00:14:48.291 --> 00:15:04.590
Idempotency: recharges are idempotent on the payment reference, so a duplicate callback never adds a second pack;. a payment success with a failed pack application goes to automatic retry, then refund (I10 §5.5 pattern).

176
00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:13.467
Retailer recharges: a retailer's app debits the retailer's wallet with the operator and credits the subscriber, with commission to the retailer.

177
00:15:13.747 --> 00:15:17.694
Retailer wallets need the same ledger discipline (I03).

178
00:15:19.044 --> 00:15:23.055
Volume: recharge peaks come around offers and validity renewals.

179
00:15:23.335 --> 00:15:28.903
The OCS provisioning path must handle tens of thousands of plan applications per second.

180
00:15:30.253 --> 00:15:32.493
Journey 4: a postpaid bill cycle.

181
00:15:32.773 --> 00:15:48.389
Usage collection: usage records from the network (data, voice, SMS, roaming) and charging records from the OCS flow through mediation (§5.5): collection, validation, deduplication, correlation, normalisation.

182
00:15:48.669 --> 00:15:55.133
Rating: each usage record is priced against the plan: included allowances first, then overage rates.

183
00:15:55.413 --> 00:16:01.749
Many postpaid plans are unlimited with fair use, so rating mostly tracks allowances and add-ons.

184
00:16:02.029 --> 00:16:15.405
Bill cycles: subscribers are spread across cycles, for example, bills generated on the 3rd, 9th, 15th, 21st and 27th of each month, so the billing workload is spread (§6.4).

185
00:16:16.755 --> 00:16:39.496
Bill run for a cycle, after the cut-off: apply recurring charges (monthly rental, prorated for mid-cycle changes; §6.5);. add one-time charges (add-ons) and usage charges;. apply discounts (family plan sharing, loyalty) and taxes (GST);. carry forward previous balance and payments;. compute due date and minimum amount if applicable.

186
00:16:40.846 --> 00:16:47.374
Invoice: invoices are rendered (PDF, itemised details on request) and sent by email and app.

187
00:16:47.654 --> 00:16:52.518
Payments come by UPI autopay, cards or net banking, and are reconciled.

188
00:16:52.798 --> 00:17:00.841
Collections: unpaid bills trigger dunning: reminders, then outgoing call barring, then full barring, then disconnection per rules.

189
00:17:01.121 --> 00:17:07.094
Credit limits prevent runaway usage mid-cycle via real-time spending checks in the OCS.

190
00:17:08.444 --> 00:17:12.519
Journey 5: new connection with eKYC and number portability.

191
00:17:12.799 --> 00:17:20.628
KYC: the customer chooses a number (from number inventory) or requests to port in an existing number, and completes KYC.

192
00:17:20.908 --> 00:17:33.537
Digital eKYC with Aadhaar-based authentication, or other permitted methods, per DoT rules (VERIFY current KYC norms, including restrictions on the number of SIMs per person).

193
00:17:33.817 --> 00:17:43.161
Order management: validates the order;. reserves the number;. allocates a SIM (ICCID or IMSI) or generates an eSIM profile;.

194
00:17:44.511 --> 00:17:45.685
applies the plan.

195
00:17:45.965 --> 00:18:00.834
Provisioning pushes the subscriber to: the HSS or UDM (identity, authentication keys, allowed services);. policy control (plan policies);. the OCS (balances, plan);. voicemail and SMS centres.

196
00:18:01.114 --> 00:18:06.789
Each is a step in an orchestrated workflow with retries and compensation (Ch 13).

197
00:18:08.139 --> 00:18:24.544
Port-in: a unique porting code from the donor operator (via SMS) goes in the porting request;. the MNP clearing house coordinates with the donor;. at the scheduled porting window, routing databases update, the donor disconnects and the recipient activates.

198
00:18:24.824 --> 00:18:27.640
Timelines and rules are regulated (VERIFY).

199
00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:32.894
Activation: confirmation to the customer, with a welcome pack.

200
00:18:33.174 --> 00:18:40.811
The SIM may be activated after verification steps required by rules (VERIFY tele-verification requirements).

201
00:18:42.161 --> 00:18:44.273
Journey 6: international roaming.

202
00:18:44.553 --> 00:18:50.825
Detach and attach: the subscriber lands in another country, and the phone attaches to a partner network.

203
00:18:51.105 --> 00:19:02.263
Home routing: for data, traffic is typically routed to the home network's gateway, so home charging applies (VERIFY architectures such as home-routed versus local breakout).

204
00:19:02.543 --> 00:19:04.697
The OCS applies the roaming pack.

205
00:19:04.977 --> 00:19:15.068
Voice via the visited network: the visited network sends usage records to the home operator through a clearing house in TAP files (Transferred Account Procedure).

206
00:19:15.348 --> 00:19:21.855
The home operator rates them for the subscriber, and settles wholesale charges with the partner per the roaming agreement.

207
00:19:23.205 --> 00:19:30.138
Bill-shock prevention: real-time roaming notifications and spending caps, required in some markets (VERIFY).

208
00:19:31.488 --> 00:19:32.448
Part 5.

209
00:19:32.728 --> 00:19:34.264
Core Subsystems in Depth.

210
00:19:35.514 --> 00:19:38.714
Online charging system (OCS or CHF).

211
00:19:38.994 --> 00:19:55.207
Functions: session management (per credit-control session);. rating (turning units into money or allowance usage);. balance management (reservations, commits, releases);. thresholds and notifications;. policy counters reported to policy control.

212
00:19:56.557 --> 00:20:05.133
The state per subscriber: balance buckets, active reservations per session and rating group, counters (daily usage), plan attributes.

213
00:20:05.413 --> 00:20:12.240
Tens of KB per subscriber, then tens of TB for 400M, held in memory across a cluster.

214
00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:16.104
Partitioning: by subscriber (IMSI or MSISDN hash).

215
00:20:16.384 --> 00:20:21.525
All requests for a subscriber go to the owning partition, which processes them serially.

216
00:20:21.805 --> 00:20:27.117
The single-writer model again (Ch 55) means no locks and no double spend.

217
00:20:28.467 --> 00:20:33.374
Shared balances: family or corporate plans share a data pool across members.

218
00:20:33.654 --> 00:20:42.230
They need either a shared-account partition (members' requests forward to the pool owner) or co-location of family members on the same partition.

219
00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:47.587
Cross-partition charging adds latency and failure modes, so avoid it where possible.

220
00:20:47.867 --> 00:20:58.811
Replication: each partition has a synchronous replica in the same site (committed state survives node loss);. an asynchronous replica in another site for disaster recovery;.

221
00:21:00.161 --> 00:21:05.047
on site failover, in-flight reservations are reconciled via final records.

222
00:21:05.327 --> 00:21:17.743
Failure behaviour: if the OCS is unreachable, the network applies Credit Control Failure Handling (terminate, or continue for a while and charge later; configured per service; VERIFY).

223
00:21:18.023 --> 00:21:25.020
Many operators allow limited continuation to protect customer experience, accepting some revenue risk.

224
00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:29.229
Policy control (PCRF or PCF).

225
00:21:29.509 --> 00:21:30.447
Decisions:

226
00:21:30.727 --> 00:21:44.893
QoS and speed per session (maximum bitrate), throttling after fair-use limits, zero-rating (some traffic not charged, where permitted; VERIFY net neutrality rules), and application-specific policies where allowed.

227
00:21:45.173 --> 00:21:52.618
Inputs: subscriber plan, usage counters from the OCS (threshold crossings), location, network congestion.

228
00:21:52.898 --> 00:22:01.751
Updates are pushed to gateways on changes, for example, a throttle at 100% daily usage, or an upgrade after an add-on purchase.

229
00:22:03.101 --> 00:22:04.296
Balance buckets.

230
00:22:04.576 --> 00:22:05.920
Here is a quick table:

231
00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:08.483
Bucket type, and Example, and Behaviour.

232
00:22:08.763 --> 00:22:10.128
Main account (money).

233
00:22:10.408 --> 00:22:11.283
Example:

234
00:22:11.563 --> 00:22:13.931
45.50 rupees.

235
00:22:14.211 --> 00:22:15.107
Behaviour:

236
00:22:15.387 --> 00:22:18.352
Used for pay-per-use; validity rules may apply.

237
00:22:18.632 --> 00:22:20.040
Daily recurring data.

238
00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:21.195
Example:

239
00:22:21.475 --> 00:22:23.693
2 GB per day for 28 days.

240
00:22:23.973 --> 00:22:24.869
Behaviour:

241
00:22:25.149 --> 00:22:28.648
Resets at midnight; unused doesn't carry over (plan terms).

242
00:22:28.928 --> 00:22:30.379
One-time data add-on.

243
00:22:30.659 --> 00:22:31.533
Example:

244
00:22:31.813 --> 00:22:34.331
10 GB, valid till plan expiry.

245
00:22:34.611 --> 00:22:35.507
Behaviour:

246
00:22:35.787 --> 00:22:38.475
Used after daily data per consumption order.

247
00:22:38.755 --> 00:22:39.544
Voice.

248
00:22:39.824 --> 00:22:40.699
Example:

249
00:22:40.979 --> 00:22:42.963
Unlimited (fair use) or minutes.

250
00:22:43.243 --> 00:22:44.139
Behaviour:

251
00:22:44.419 --> 00:22:45.784
Counters for fair use.

252
00:22:46.064 --> 00:22:46.981
SMS.

253
00:22:47.261 --> 00:22:48.136
Example:

254
00:22:48.416 --> 00:22:49.717
100 per day.

255
00:22:49.997 --> 00:22:50.893
Behaviour:

256
00:22:51.173 --> 00:22:52.240
Resets daily.

257
00:22:52.520 --> 00:22:53.501
Roaming pack.

258
00:22:53.781 --> 00:22:54.656
Example:

259
00:22:54.936 --> 00:22:57.432
5 GB in the UAE for 7 days.

260
00:22:57.712 --> 00:22:58.608
Behaviour:

261
00:22:58.888 --> 00:23:01.021
Applies only in specific countries.

262
00:23:02.371 --> 00:23:03.651
Shared family pool.

263
00:23:03.931 --> 00:23:04.806
Example:

264
00:23:05.086 --> 00:23:07.134
100 GB shared among 4.

265
00:23:07.414 --> 00:23:08.310
Behaviour:

266
00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:10.105
Forwarded to pool owner.

267
00:23:11.455 --> 00:23:23.871
Consumption order (§6.2): which bucket is used first when several can pay for the same usage, for example, daily data before add-on data, and add-ons expiring soonest first.

268
00:23:25.221 --> 00:23:28.719
Periodic processes on 400M subscribers.

269
00:23:28.999 --> 00:23:34.567
Daily resets, expiries, renewals: millions of subscribers' buckets reset at midnight.

270
00:23:34.847 --> 00:23:39.925
Don't do it as a batch at 00:00, which creates a charging spike.

271
00:23:40.205 --> 00:23:50.935
Instead: lazy reset: each bucket stores its period, and the first charging request after midnight resets it in-line;. or a staggered job per partition over minutes.

272
00:23:51.215 --> 00:23:59.855
The throttle must lift at midnight for policy correctness, so policy control handles the time-based rule itself or receives staggered updates.

273
00:24:01.205 --> 00:24:13.237
Plan expiry: validity end triggers the grace period rules, reduced services (incoming only), and final deactivation after a long period (VERIFY rules on number recycling).

274
00:24:14.587 --> 00:24:15.590
Mediation.

275
00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:23.849
Collection: usage records arrive from gateways, IMS, SMSC and OCS, as files or streams in vendor formats.

276
00:24:24.129 --> 00:24:26.945
Validation: mandatory fields, ranges.

277
00:24:27.225 --> 00:24:31.811
Deduplication: the same record delivered twice (§6.6).

278
00:24:32.091 --> 00:24:42.011
Correlation: long sessions produce partial records (for example, every hour or every N MB), which are joined into sessions for billing and analysis.

279
00:24:42.291 --> 00:24:44.467
Normalisation to a common format.

280
00:24:44.747 --> 00:24:49.483
Enrichment with subscriber and plan data, cell location, and roaming partner.

281
00:24:50.833 --> 00:24:57.575
Routing to postpaid rating, interconnect billing, the fraud system, the data lake and regulatory retention.

282
00:24:57.855 --> 00:25:05.855
Reliability: every record is counted in and out (record count and volume reconciliation), which feeds revenue assurance.

283
00:25:07.205 --> 00:25:09.103
Rating and billing (postpaid).

284
00:25:09.383 --> 00:25:19.389
Rating engine: tariff trees (§6.3), with plan-specific allowances, overage rates, time-of-day rates, zone rates for roaming, and discounts.

285
00:25:19.669 --> 00:25:31.487
Billing engine: bill cycles; recurring, one-time and usage charges; proration; adjustments and credits; taxes; invoice generation; payment allocation; account balances.

286
00:25:31.767 --> 00:25:38.487
Hierarchies: corporate accounts with many subscribers, cost centres, pooled usage, consolidated invoices.

287
00:25:39.837 --> 00:25:50.568
Re-rating: if a tariff error is found, affected usage is re-rated before the bill run (or with adjustments afterwards), so rating must be repeatable from stored usage.

288
00:25:51.918 --> 00:25:53.838
Interconnect and roaming settlement.

289
00:25:54.118 --> 00:26:05.062
Interconnect: when your subscriber calls another operator's subscriber, you owe them a termination charge (as set by the regulator; VERIFY current interconnect usage charges).

290
00:26:05.342 --> 00:26:15.539
Mediation produces interconnect records, which are aggregated per partner per period, reconciled with the partner's statements (disputes on differences), and settled.

291
00:26:15.819 --> 00:26:16.630
Roaming:

292
00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:20.003
TAP files exchanged via clearing houses.

293
00:26:20.283 --> 00:26:22.011
Wholesale rates per agreement.

294
00:26:22.291 --> 00:26:28.286
Fraud control on high-usage roamers (via near-real-time roaming exchange formats; VERIFY).

295
00:26:29.636 --> 00:26:31.343
Revenue assurance and fraud.

296
00:26:31.623 --> 00:26:52.145
Revenue assurance reconciles each hand-off: network usage versus mediation input;. mediation output versus billed usage;. provisioning in the network versus subscriptions in BSS (provisioned but not billed services);. recharges paid versus packs applied;. interconnect traffic versus partner invoices.

297
00:26:53.495 --> 00:26:58.594
Leakage is typically a small percentage of revenue, which is huge in absolute terms.

298
00:26:58.874 --> 00:26:59.941
Fraud types:

299
00:27:00.221 --> 00:27:10.759
SIM box or bypass fraud: international calls routed over the internet and terminated through local SIMs to avoid international termination charges;.

300
00:27:11.039 --> 00:27:18.997
IRSF (international revenue share fraud): calls to premium international numbers from compromised accounts or SIMs;.

301
00:27:19.277 --> 00:27:23.031
Wangiri: missed calls from premium numbers prompting callbacks;.

302
00:27:24.381 --> 00:27:25.704
subscription fraud:

303
00:27:25.984 --> 00:27:28.309
SIMs obtained with fake KYC;.

304
00:27:28.589 --> 00:27:36.163
SIM swap fraud: taking over someone's number to intercept OTPs, a banking fraud vector (I03).

305
00:27:36.443 --> 00:27:38.939
Detection is in §6.7.

306
00:27:40.289 --> 00:27:41.227
Part 6.

307
00:27:41.507 --> 00:27:42.467
Algorithms.

308
00:27:43.717 --> 00:27:44.784
Quota sizing.

309
00:27:45.064 --> 00:28:01.939
Trade-off: small quotas: many requests (load), little risk of overuse;. large quotas: few requests, but a subscriber can overuse by up to one quota when the balance hits zero, and multiple concurrent sessions each hold reservations, fragmenting the balance.

310
00:28:02.219 --> 00:28:07.552
Adaptive quotas: grant based on remaining balance (never more than a fraction of what's left);.

311
00:28:08.902 --> 00:28:18.459
the subscriber's recent usage rate (so a video streamer gets bigger quotas, and a chat user smaller ones);. the number of concurrent sessions;. service type.

312
00:28:18.739 --> 00:28:27.827
Near exhaustion: grant small quotas as the balance nears zero, or a final unit indication telling the gateway to redirect after this grant.

313
00:28:28.107 --> 00:28:34.401
Validity timers force periodic re-authorisation, for plan changes and for fairness across sessions.

314
00:28:35.751 --> 00:28:37.287
Bucket consumption order.

315
00:28:37.567 --> 00:28:38.420
Rules:

316
00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:44.119
Eligible buckets for this service and location (for example, roaming pack only abroad).

317
00:28:44.399 --> 00:28:48.004
Then priority by type (daily allowance before paid add-on).

318
00:28:48.284 --> 00:28:51.143
Then earliest expiry first, to avoid waste.

319
00:28:51.423 --> 00:28:54.111
Then the main account (money) last, if allowed.

320
00:28:55.461 --> 00:28:59.941
Implementation: buckets sorted by (priority, expiry) per subscriber.

321
00:29:00.221 --> 00:29:06.279
A reservation may span buckets: take the rest from the next bucket when one runs out mid-reservation.

322
00:29:07.629 --> 00:29:09.059
Rating with tariff trees.

323
00:29:09.339 --> 00:29:26.363
A tariff tree: a decision tree over the attributes of a usage event: service (data or voice or SMS);. destination type (on-net, off-net, international, premium);. time band;. location (home, national roaming, international zone);. plan and add-ons.

324
00:29:26.643 --> 00:29:32.360
Leaves give a price or allowance counter and a rounding rule (per second, per minute, per 10 KB).

325
00:29:33.710 --> 00:29:42.947
Performance: trees are compiled from the catalogue into efficient lookup structures (prefix tries for destination number prefixes, time band tables).

326
00:29:43.227 --> 00:29:45.809
Rating happens in microseconds per event.

327
00:29:46.089 --> 00:29:52.723
Number prefix matching: longest-prefix match on dialled numbers for international destinations (a trie).

328
00:29:54.073 --> 00:29:55.588
Bill cycle partitioning.

329
00:29:55.868 --> 00:30:01.116
Assign subscribers to cycles to balance workload: hash-based, or by signup date.

330
00:30:01.396 --> 00:30:03.977
Customers can move cycles, with proration.

331
00:30:04.257 --> 00:30:08.183
Within a cycle: the bill run parallelises by account partitions.

332
00:30:08.463 --> 00:30:11.769
Dependencies (corporate hierarchies) are processed together.

333
00:30:12.049 --> 00:30:16.252
Deadlines: bills must be issued within set days after the cycle close.

334
00:30:16.532 --> 00:30:19.433
The monitoring target is the bill run's end time.

335
00:30:20.783 --> 00:30:21.701
Proration.

336
00:30:21.981 --> 00:30:27.975
Mid-cycle plan change: charge the old plan for the days used and the new plan for the remaining days.

337
00:30:28.255 --> 00:30:37.898
The formula is (days active ÷ days in cycle) times monthly charge, by the product's proration rule: daily, or no proration for some.

338
00:30:38.178 --> 00:30:41.527
Allowances are prorated similarly or per product rules.

339
00:30:41.807 --> 00:30:46.394
Rounding per rules, and consistent between rating and invoice presentation.

340
00:30:47.744 --> 00:30:50.219
Record deduplication and correlation.

341
00:30:50.499 --> 00:30:57.795
Deduplication: each record has identifiers (node ID, sequence number, session ID, record opening time).

342
00:30:58.075 --> 00:31:09.232
Mediation keeps a dedup window of seen record keys, using Bloom filters plus exact stores for recent windows (Ch 53 L2 for Bloom filter trade-offs).

343
00:31:09.512 --> 00:31:13.885
Late duplicates beyond the window are caught by later reconciliation.

344
00:31:14.165 --> 00:31:20.779
Correlation: join partial records of the same session (by session ID and charging ID) in order.

345
00:31:21.059 --> 00:31:26.392
Handle missing partials: after a timeout, flag and estimate, or wait for re-delivery.

346
00:31:27.742 --> 00:31:28.830
Fraud detection.

347
00:31:29.110 --> 00:31:30.454
Here is a quick table:

348
00:31:30.734 --> 00:31:32.590
Fraud, and Signals and method.

349
00:31:32.870 --> 00:31:33.809
SIM box:

350
00:31:34.089 --> 00:31:50.110
SIMs with many outgoing calls to many distinct numbers, almost no incoming calls, no data usage, stationary location, high call volumes at odd hours, then rules + classifiers; test-call programmes to catch bypass routes.

351
00:31:50.390 --> 00:31:51.649
IRSF or Wangiri:

352
00:31:51.929 --> 00:32:04.217
Sudden calls to high-cost international ranges; known fraud number ranges from industry databases; velocity rules per subscriber and per destination prefix; near-real-time blocking.

353
00:32:04.497 --> 00:32:05.819
Subscription fraud:

354
00:32:06.099 --> 00:32:12.051
KYC anomalies, many SIMs per identity or device, immediate high-risk usage.

355
00:32:12.331 --> 00:32:13.483
SIM swap:

356
00:32:13.763 --> 00:32:27.907
SIM change followed by banking OTP traffic; the operator's SIM swap API or signal is shared with banks where available (VERIFY) and a cooling period on SMS after swap (VERIFY).

357
00:32:29.257 --> 00:32:35.252
Graph analysis over calling patterns finds clusters of SIMs controlled by one fraudster.

358
00:32:36.602 --> 00:32:38.138
Churn and next-best-offer.

359
00:32:38.418 --> 00:32:53.287
Churn prediction features: recharge history (gaps, amounts), usage decline, network quality experienced (dropped calls, slow data in their areas), complaints, port-out requests (a strong last-minute signal), competitor offers.

360
00:32:53.567 --> 00:33:02.271
Next best offer: recommend packs based on usage patterns (data-heavy versus voice-heavy), price sensitivity and time to expiry.

361
00:33:02.551 --> 00:33:07.181
Contextual bandits test offers (I06 §6.6).

362
00:33:08.531 --> 00:33:09.469
Part 7.

363
00:33:09.749 --> 00:33:11.072
Data Architecture.

364
