480
00:38:35.257 --> 00:38:36.921
Increasingly containerised.

481
00:38:38.271 --> 00:38:39.167
Part 9.

482
00:38:39.447 --> 00:38:41.687
Security, Privacy and Compliance.

483
00:38:42.937 --> 00:38:44.153
Subscriber data:

484
00:38:44.433 --> 00:38:49.574
KYC documents, location, usage and call records are highly sensitive.

485
00:38:49.854 --> 00:38:55.550
Strict access control, encryption and audit, and DPDP Act obligations (VERIFY).

486
00:38:55.830 --> 00:39:09.035
Lawful interception and data retention: licence conditions require lawful interception capabilities and retention of usage records for set periods, provided only under legal process (VERIFY).

487
00:39:09.315 --> 00:39:22.969
KYC and SIM issuance rules: limits on SIMs per individual, verification requirements for point-of-sale agents, and rules against bulk SIM sales (VERIFY current DoT rules).

488
00:39:24.319 --> 00:39:35.220
Tariffs and consumer protection: tariff filing and transparency, validity and plan disclosure rules, and quality of service regulations (VERIFY TRAI regulations).

489
00:39:35.500 --> 00:39:49.196
Anti-spam: commercial communication rules and registration of senders and headers on distributed ledger platforms (the TRAI TCCCPR framework; VERIFY), and spam call and SMS blocking.

490
00:39:49.476 --> 00:39:50.735
Network security:

491
00:39:51.015 --> 00:39:58.183
SS7 or Diameter signalling firewalls against location tracking and fraud; SIM swap protections.

492
00:39:59.533 --> 00:40:00.386
Part 10.

493
00:40:00.666 --> 00:40:02.586
Reliability and Operations.

494
00:40:03.836 --> 00:40:18.364
Charging availability: geographically redundant OCS sites;. synchronous replicas within a site;. tested failover;. configured failure handling at gateways (limited continuation) so outages don't drop all traffic.

495
00:40:18.644 --> 00:40:24.383
Avoid synchronised spikes: daily resets, expiries and renewals are lazy or staggered.

496
00:40:24.663 --> 00:40:27.671
Recharge promotions have pre-scaled capacity.

497
00:40:29.021 --> 00:40:35.655
Catalogue releases: new plans are tested with rating test cases and pushed to OCS and billing in sync.

498
00:40:35.935 --> 00:40:40.863
A mismatch between OCS and billing definitions is a classic source of disputes.

499
00:40:41.143 --> 00:40:51.639
Bill runs: monitored per cycle (records rated, exceptions, run time), with a bill verification sample before sending (automated checks against expectations).

500
00:40:51.919 --> 00:40:56.869
Revenue assurance dashboards: daily reconciliations with thresholds and alerts.

501
00:40:58.219 --> 00:40:59.776
Festival and event peaks:

502
00:41:00.056 --> 00:41:08.611
New Year's greetings (voice and SMS peaks), cricket matches (data peaks), and the recharge surge when many plans expire together.

503
00:41:09.961 --> 00:41:10.963
Part 11.

504
00:41:11.243 --> 00:41:12.289
Key Trade-offs.

505
00:41:13.539 --> 00:41:14.883
Here is a quick table:

506
00:41:15.163 --> 00:41:17.680
Decision, and Chosen, and Accepted cost.

507
00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:19.112
Charging model.

508
00:41:19.392 --> 00:41:20.224
Chosen:

509
00:41:20.504 --> 00:41:22.147
Reservation-based quotas.

510
00:41:22.427 --> 00:41:23.600
Accepted cost:

511
00:41:23.880 --> 00:41:26.973
Load versus overuse risk tuned via quota size.

512
00:41:27.253 --> 00:41:28.341
Balance store.

513
00:41:28.621 --> 00:41:29.453
Chosen:

514
00:41:29.733 --> 00:41:31.909
In-memory, partitioned single writer.

515
00:41:32.189 --> 00:41:33.363
Accepted cost:

516
00:41:33.643 --> 00:41:35.797
Complex replication and failover.

517
00:41:36.077 --> 00:41:37.101
Shared plans.

518
00:41:37.381 --> 00:41:38.213
Chosen:

519
00:41:38.493 --> 00:41:39.859
Pool owner partition.

520
00:41:40.139 --> 00:41:41.312
Accepted cost:

521
00:41:41.592 --> 00:41:43.085
Extra hop for members.

522
00:41:43.365 --> 00:41:44.240
Resets.

523
00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:45.352
Chosen:

524
00:41:45.632 --> 00:41:47.104
Lazy per subscriber.

525
00:41:47.384 --> 00:41:48.557
Accepted cost:

526
00:41:48.837 --> 00:41:50.821
Logic spread into charging path.

527
00:41:51.101 --> 00:41:52.296
Failure handling.

528
00:41:52.576 --> 00:41:53.408
Chosen:

529
00:41:53.688 --> 00:41:55.224
Limited continuation.

530
00:41:55.504 --> 00:41:56.677
Accepted cost:

531
00:41:56.957 --> 00:41:59.091
Some revenue risk during outages.

532
00:41:59.371 --> 00:42:00.437
Usage data.

533
00:42:00.717 --> 00:42:01.549
Chosen:

534
00:42:01.829 --> 00:42:03.344
Lake with huge retention.

535
00:42:03.624 --> 00:42:04.797
Accepted cost:

536
00:42:05.077 --> 00:42:07.531
Storage cost; strict access controls.

537
00:42:07.811 --> 00:42:08.664
Catalogue.

538
00:42:08.944 --> 00:42:09.776
Chosen:

539
00:42:10.056 --> 00:42:11.827
Central, versioned, compiled.

540
00:42:12.107 --> 00:42:13.280
Accepted cost:

541
00:42:13.560 --> 00:42:15.480
Release discipline across systems.

542
00:42:16.830 --> 00:42:17.769
Part 12.

543
00:42:18.049 --> 00:42:19.393
Interview Q and A.

544
00:42:20.643 --> 00:42:21.475
Beginner.

545
00:42:21.755 --> 00:42:22.779
Question 1.

546
00:42:23.059 --> 00:42:26.173
How does a prepaid phone's data get charged in real time?

547
00:42:26.453 --> 00:42:27.819
Thirty-second answer:

548
00:42:28.099 --> 00:42:50.605
The network gateway asks the online charging system for a quota before letting data flow; the OCS reserves some of the subscriber's balance and grants it; the gateway reports usage when the quota is used or a timer expires; the OCS commits the usage, releases leftovers and grants more, or denies and the gateway redirects to a recharge page.

549
00:42:51.955 --> 00:42:52.958
Question 2.

550
00:42:53.238 --> 00:42:55.777
What is the difference between OCS and billing?

551
00:42:56.057 --> 00:42:57.422
Thirty-second answer:

552
00:42:57.702 --> 00:43:13.979
The OCS authorises and deducts usage in real time against balances (prepaid, and postpaid credit limits); billing prices usage records and recurring charges into periodic invoices for postpaid, with taxes, payments and collections.

553
00:43:15.329 --> 00:43:16.375
Question 3.

554
00:43:16.655 --> 00:43:18.980
What happens when you recharge with UPI?

555
00:43:19.260 --> 00:43:20.625
Thirty-second answer:

556
00:43:20.905 --> 00:43:36.457
Payment succeeds via the payment orchestration; the recharge service idempotently applies the pack to the OCS (new buckets and validity), updates policy if speeds change, and notifies you, retrying or refunding if the pack can't be applied.

557
00:43:37.807 --> 00:43:38.874
Question 4.

558
00:43:39.154 --> 00:43:41.885
Why does data speed drop after the daily limit?

559
00:43:42.165 --> 00:43:43.530
Thirty-second answer:

560
00:43:43.810 --> 00:43:54.967
The plan's fair usage policy: when the daily high-speed allowance is used, the OCS tells policy control, which lowers the session's maximum speed until the daily reset.

561
00:43:56.317 --> 00:43:57.320
Intermediate.

562
00:43:57.600 --> 00:43:58.709
Question 5.

563
00:43:58.989 --> 00:44:01.677
How would you size data quotas in the OCS?

564
00:44:01.957 --> 00:44:03.323
Thirty-second answer:

565
00:44:03.603 --> 00:44:21.288
Adaptively: a fraction of remaining balance, larger for heavy users and smaller near exhaustion, adjusted for concurrent sessions and service type, with validity timers and a final-unit indication near zero, balancing request load against overuse and stranded reservations.

566
00:44:22.638 --> 00:44:23.747
Question 6.

567
00:44:24.027 --> 00:44:26.481
In what order should balance buckets be consumed?

568
00:44:26.761 --> 00:44:28.126
Thirty-second answer:

569
00:44:28.406 --> 00:44:41.974
Filter eligible buckets for the service and location, then by priority (daily allowances before paid add-ons), then earliest expiry first, with the main money balance last; reservations may span buckets.

570
00:44:43.324 --> 00:44:44.412
Question 7.

571
00:44:44.692 --> 00:44:46.079
What does mediation do?

572
00:44:46.359 --> 00:44:47.724
Thirty-second answer:

573
00:44:48.004 --> 00:45:05.177
Collects usage records from network elements, validates, deduplicates, correlates partial records into sessions, normalises and enriches them, and routes them to billing, settlement, fraud, the data lake and retention, with record-count reconciliation for revenue assurance.

574
00:45:06.527 --> 00:45:07.551
Question 8.

575
00:45:07.831 --> 00:45:09.730
How is a postpaid bill produced?

576
00:45:10.010 --> 00:45:11.375
Thirty-second answer:

577
00:45:11.655 --> 00:45:28.487
Usage is rated against plan allowances and overages; at the cycle cut-off, the bill run adds prorated recurring charges, one-time charges, usage, discounts, taxes and previous balances, then generates invoices and handles payments and collections.

578
00:45:29.837 --> 00:45:30.904
Question 9.

579
00:45:31.184 --> 00:45:32.869
How does number portability work?

580
00:45:33.149 --> 00:45:34.515
Thirty-second answer:

581
00:45:34.795 --> 00:45:52.181
The customer gets a unique porting code from the donor, the recipient submits a port request through the MNP clearing house, the donor validates, and at the porting window routing databases update, the donor disconnects and the recipient activates via provisioning (VERIFY current rules).

582
00:45:53.531 --> 00:45:54.299
Senior.

583
00:45:54.579 --> 00:45:55.625
Question 10.

584
00:45:55.905 --> 00:45:59.361
Design an OCS for 400 million subscribers.

585
00:45:59.641 --> 00:46:01.006
Thirty-second answer:

586
00:46:01.286 --> 00:46:21.958
In-memory, partitioned by subscriber, single writer per partition, synchronous replica in-site and async across sites; Diameter or HTTP2 front ends; reservation-based quotas with adaptive sizing; bucket consumption rules; threshold events to policy and notifications; lazy daily resets; shared pools

587
00:46:22.238 --> 00:46:25.993
via owner partitions; failure handling agreed with the network.

588
00:46:27.343 --> 00:46:28.473
Question 11.

589
00:46:28.753 --> 00:46:31.505
How do you avoid the midnight spike from daily resets?

590
00:46:31.785 --> 00:46:33.151
Thirty-second answer:

591
00:46:33.431 --> 00:46:47.105
Store each bucket's period and reset lazily on the first charging request after the boundary, or stagger resets per partition; handle the throttle lift via time-based policy rules rather than a mass policy push.

592
00:46:48.455 --> 00:46:49.543
Question 12.

593
00:46:49.823 --> 00:46:52.255
How would you detect SIM box fraud?

594
00:46:52.535 --> 00:46:53.901
Thirty-second answer:

595
00:46:54.181 --> 00:47:13.765
Profile SIMs from usage streams, many outgoing calls to many numbers, few incoming, no data, static location, odd-hour volumes, with rules and classifiers, graph analysis for SIM clusters, and test-call programmes that detect bypass routes; block quickly and share intelligence.

596
00:47:15.115 --> 00:47:16.395
Question 13.

597
00:47:16.675 --> 00:47:19.448
What is revenue assurance and how do you implement it?

598
00:47:19.728 --> 00:47:21.093
Thirty-second answer:

599
00:47:21.373 --> 00:47:38.717
Systematic reconciliation of every hand-off, network versus mediation, mediation versus billing, provisioned versus billed services, payments versus packs applied, interconnect versus partner invoices, with thresholds, alerts and root-cause workflows to find leakage.

600
00:47:40.067 --> 00:47:41.262
Question 14.

601
00:47:41.542 --> 00:47:45.019
How do you process tens of billions of usage records a day?

602
00:47:45.299 --> 00:47:46.665
Thirty-second answer:

603
00:47:46.945 --> 00:48:04.587
Mediation streams into Kafka partitioned by subscriber; Flink for real-time features; columnar lake tables partitioned by date and hour; Spark and Trino or ClickHouse for analysis; pre-aggregated marts; compression and tiered retention with strict access controls.

604
00:48:05.937 --> 00:48:06.833
Architect.

605
00:48:07.113 --> 00:48:08.308
Question 15.

606
00:48:08.588 --> 00:48:11.681
Design the BSS of a large mobile operator.

607
00:48:11.961 --> 00:48:13.327
Thirty-second answer:

608
00:48:13.607 --> 00:48:34.300
CRM and self-care, a versioned product catalogue feeding OCS and billing, order management with provisioning orchestration to HSS or UDM, policy and OCS, converged real-time charging, recharges and payments, mediation, postpaid rating and billing, collections, interconnect and roaming settlement,

609
00:48:34.580 --> 00:48:41.407
revenue assurance and fraud, and a usage data platform, aligned with TM Forum models and APIs.

610
00:48:42.757 --> 00:48:43.951
Question 16.

611
00:48:44.231 --> 00:48:48.434
How do you keep the catalogue consistent across OCS, billing and apps?

612
00:48:48.714 --> 00:48:50.079
Thirty-second answer:

613
00:48:50.359 --> 00:49:08.002
One versioned catalogue as the source of truth, compiled into runtime artefacts for OCS and billing, released together with automated rating test cases, apps reading eligibility and prices from catalogue APIs, and monitoring for mismatches between charged and billed amounts.

614
00:49:09.352 --> 00:49:10.696
Question 17.

615
00:49:10.976 --> 00:49:13.621
What should happen when the OCS is unavailable?

616
00:49:13.901 --> 00:49:15.267
Thirty-second answer:

617
00:49:15.547 --> 00:49:33.979
The network applies configured credit control failure handling, often allowing sessions to continue for a limited time or volume and charging later from records, while the OCS fails over to replicas or another site; the business agrees the revenue-versus-experience trade-off in advance.

618
00:49:35.329 --> 00:49:36.502
Question 18.

619
00:49:36.782 --> 00:49:41.241
How would you migrate from a legacy billing system to a new converged platform?

620
00:49:41.521 --> 00:49:42.886
Thirty-second answer:

621
00:49:43.166 --> 00:49:58.163
Migrate by segment (new subscribers first, then plan families), run parallel rating to compare outcomes, reconcile balances at cut-over, keep the catalogue as the translation layer, and plan around bill cycles and recharge peaks.

622
00:49:59.513 --> 00:50:00.751
Question 19.

623
00:50:01.031 --> 00:50:04.423
How do regulations shape telecom BSS in India?

624
00:50:04.703 --> 00:50:06.068
Thirty-second answer:

625
00:50:06.348 --> 00:50:27.617
KYC and SIM issuance rules shape onboarding; MNP rules shape porting workflows; tariff filing and transparency shape the catalogue; lawful interception and retention shape usage data handling; anti-spam frameworks shape messaging; quality of service and reporting shape analytics (VERIFY current regulations).

626
00:50:28.967 --> 00:50:30.077
Question 20.

627
00:50:30.357 --> 00:50:33.621
Compare telecom charging with banking and payments systems.

628
00:50:33.901 --> 00:50:35.266
Thirty-second answer:

629
00:50:35.546 --> 00:50:58.799
Both keep strongly consistent balances with double-spend protection and idempotency; telecom charges continuous usage via reservations at millions of TPS with network timers, while banking moves discrete amounts with regulatory settlement deadlines, telecom favours in-memory partitioned stores; banking favours durable ledgers with reconciliation.

630
00:51:00.149 --> 00:51:01.195
Part 13.

631
00:51:01.475 --> 00:51:02.243
Summary.

632
00:51:03.493 --> 00:51:15.226
Real-time charging is reservation-based: the network asks for quotas, and the OCS reserves, commits and releases against balance buckets at millions of transactions per second.

633
00:51:15.506 --> 00:51:22.631
The OCS is an in-memory, subscriber-partitioned, single-writer system with replicas and agreed failure handling.

634
00:51:22.911 --> 00:51:25.983
Resets and expiries are lazy to avoid spikes.

635
00:51:26.263 --> 00:51:30.061
Policy control turns usage thresholds into speed changes.

636
00:51:30.341 --> 00:51:35.162
The catalogue is the single definition of plans for charging, billing and apps.

637
00:51:36.512 --> 00:51:44.811
Postpaid billing is mediation, then rating, then cycles, then invoices, then collections, with proration, re-rating and taxes.

638
00:51:45.091 --> 00:51:47.885
Revenue assurance and fraud watch every hand-off.

639
00:51:48.165 --> 00:51:54.821
The usage data platform handles tens of billions of records per day under strict retention and access rules.

